<block-guide name="core/video"><![CDATA[
CORE/VIDEO
A video file from the media library played by the browser's own player. A YouTube or Vimeo
address is core/embed, not this block.

MARKUP (checked in the editor)
<!-- wp:video {"id":<id>} -->
<figure class="wp-block-video"><video controls src="<url>"></video><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">Our studio in one minute.</figcaption></figure>
<!-- /wp:video -->
<!-- wp:video {"id":<id>,"align":"wide"} -->
<figure class="wp-block-video alignwide"><video autoplay loop muted poster="<image url>" preload="auto" src="<url>" playsinline></video></figure>
<!-- /wp:video -->
<id> and <url> come from list-media {kind:"video"} or upload-media. The id goes ONLY in the
comment: there is no wp-video class. src is the file's url.

PLAYER SETTINGS ARE TAG ATTRIBUTES, NOT COMMENT ATTRIBUTES
autoplay, controls, loop, muted, poster, preload, playsinline are read from the <video> tag.
Write them bare (controls, not controls="true"), in this order: autoplay, controls, loop, muted,
poster, preload, src, playsinline. No controls attribute = no player controls (background video).
preload "metadata" is the default and is not written; "auto" or "none" is.

ON THE PAGE
With a real attachment id, WordPress adds the file's width, height and an aspect-ratio style
to the <video> when the page is shown, so the space is kept while it loads. Without an id the
tag is served as written.
Caption: <figcaption class="wp-element-caption"> after the video; no caption = no element.

CHECK
Open the page and play the video; reopen the editor: no invalid-content notice.
]]></block-guide>
